course: everyday
lesson: 71
title: Wild animals, zoos and kindness to animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Wild animals live their own lives and are not pets or toys: the kind thing is to watch them calmly from a safe distance and let them be.
explain: At a zoo or an aquarium the animals are still wild, so we follow the signs, stay behind the barriers, keep quiet and switch off the camera flash.
explain: Many things people believe about animals are myths: bats are not blind, owls cannot turn their heads all the way round, and sharks very rarely attack people.
explain: Kindness also means thinking about what we leave behind: a released balloon, a lost fishing hook or an uncut plastic drink ring can trap or kill an animal far away.
explain: A girl on a beach in Cornwall sees a seal resting on the sand; she keeps her dog on its lead, stays well back and watches quietly, and the seal sleeps on in peace.
fact: Seals rest on beaches and can bite, so people stay at least 10 metres away and keep dogs on a lead.
fact: Bats can see, and many bats also find their way by echoes, which is called echolocation.
fact: An owl cannot move its eyes, so it turns its head, but only about three quarters of the way round.
fact: Zoo animals are wild animals and can be dangerous, so visitors stay behind barriers and never climb fences.
fact: Banging on glass, shouting and camera flashes frighten and stress animals.
fact: In an aquarium touch pool you touch the creatures gently with two fingers, as the staff show you.
fact: Sharks rarely attack people, because people are not their normal food.
fact: Hurting animals on purpose is cruelty; tell a trusted adult, who can report it to an animal welfare organisation.
fact: An adult can call the local animal shelter about a cat or dog that seems abandoned, and the shelter can find its owner or a new home.
fact: A magnifying glass in sunshine focuses heat that burns insects, so it is never used on living things.
fact: Fishing line, hooks, released balloons and plastic drink rings can tangle animals or be eaten by them.
fact: Chasing pigeons or ducks frightens and tires them, so we watch them calmly.
fact: Zoo animals eat special food, and people's food such as chips can make them ill, so visitors never feed them.
fact: Swans and many penguins pair up as a couple, and swans often stay together for life.
fact: A family group of lions living together is called a pride.
exercise: A boy in Devon finds a seal lying on the beach. What should he do? | Stay well back, keep his dog on a lead and tell an adult if it looks hurt | Go close and stroke its head gently so it feels safe and knows he is a friend | Push it gently back into the sea | Let his dog bark so the seal swims away
exercise: Is it true that bats are blind? | No, bats can see, and many also use echoes to find their way | Yes, all bats are completely blind and find their way only by smell | Yes, bats only see in the daytime | No, bats see only colours, not shapes
exercise: Why does an owl turn its head so far? | It cannot move its eyes in their sockets | It is checking that its neck still works properly | It has a second pair of eyes on the back of its head | It can spin its head all the way round and back
exercise: What should you do at the zoo? | Follow the signs and stay behind the barriers | Climb the fence to get a better photo | Feed the animals some of your own snacks from home | Reach through the bars to touch them
exercise: Why should you not bang on the glass of an animal's enclosure? | The noise stresses and frightens the animal | The glass will break the first time | The animal cannot hear anything through glass | It makes the animal come closer to play
exercise: Why should you turn off the flash when photographing animals? | Bright flashes can frighten them, especially in dark houses | Flash photos always come out blurry | The flash uses up the zoo's electricity and the lights in the cages go out | Animals cannot be seen in flash photos
exercise: How should you touch a starfish in an aquarium touch pool? | Gently with two fingers, as the staff show | Pick it up and hold it out of the water for a while | Poke it to see if it moves | Squeeze it to feel how hard it is
exercise: Why do sharks rarely attack people? | People are not their normal food | Sharks are afraid of all humans in the water | Sharks cannot see anything near the surface | Sharks only eat plants and seaweed on the sea floor
exercise: You see an older child kicking a dog on purpose. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult about it | Kick the older child back hard | Pretend you did not see it at all | Take the dog home with you and keep it
exercise: A thin, lonely cat has been sitting outside your block for days. What is best? | Tell an adult, who can call the local animal shelter | Take it inside and keep it secretly in your bedroom | Chase it away so it finds its home | Leave it, because cats always manage
exercise: Why must you never point a magnifying glass at bugs in the sun? | It focuses the heat and burns them | It only makes them look bigger, so it does no harm | It makes them run faster | It helps them warm up after night
exercise: You find fishing line and a hook on a riverbank. What should you do? | Tell an adult or put them safely in a bin | Leave them exactly where they are | Throw them into the river | Hang them up high on a tree branch out of the way
exercise: Why should balloons not be released into the sky? | They fall back down and animals eat them or get tangled | They fly all the way up to space and pollute it for the astronauts | They pop and frighten the clouds | Released balloons are against the law everywhere
exercise: Why do people cut plastic drink rings before throwing them away? | Animals can get their heads or beaks stuck in the rings | Cut plastic takes up much less room in the bin, so bins fill up slower | Uncut rings cannot be recycled at all | It stops the rings from blowing away
exercise: What is the kind way to act around ducks in a park? | Watch them calmly without chasing them | Run straight at them to make them all fly off | Throw stones near them to see them move | Chase them so they get some good exercise
test: A girl at a zoo in San Diego wants to get closer to the tigers. What should she do? | Stay behind the barrier and watch from there | Climb the low fence while the keeper is not looking | Lean right over the wall to call out to the tigers | Wave some food at them through the bars of the cage
test: Which is true about owls? | They turn their heads about three quarters of the way round | They can turn their heads all the way round in a full circle, and more | They move their eyes instead of their heads | They cannot turn their heads at all
test: In the dark bat house a boy's camera flash keeps going off. What should he do? | Turn the flash off, because it frightens the animals | Keep taking photos, as the bats are blind anyway and cannot see it | Use a brighter torch instead | Bang on the glass to wake the bats first
test: A friend says swimming at the beach is crazy because sharks hunt people. What is true? | Shark attacks are rare, people are not their food | Sharks hunt people near every beach in the world | Sharks attack anyone who splashes | Sharks only attack children
test: What could happen to a party balloon let go in Texas? | It may come down far away and harm a bird or turtle | It will float high in the sky for ever and never come down at all | It will turn into rain | It will land back where it started
test: What do bats use besides their eyes to find their way at night? | Echoes of the sounds they make | The light of the Moon and the stars only | The smell of the trees and flowers | The warmth rising from the ground below
test: Why is the touch pool rule «two fingers, gently» important? | Sea creatures are delicate and rough hands can hurt them | The creatures will bite a whole hand | The water is too cold for whole hands | The staff count fingers to see exactly who has touched each animal
test: A boy on the beach in Wales sees a seal lying still and bleeding a little. What is best? | Keep back and tell an adult, who can call for help | Carry it back into the water himself as quickly as he can | Throw his towel over it and lift it | Poke it with a stick to see if it is alive
test: Which action is unkind to animals even if you do not mean harm? | Leaving fishing line lying by a pond | Watching ducks quietly from a park bench | Cutting drink rings before binning them | Telling an adult about a hungry stray dog
test: seal on the beach!! can we go up close for a selfie | No, stay at least 10 metres away, it can bite | Yes, seals are friendly and tame, they love people | Yes, if you move very slowly | Yes, if the dog stays with you
test: monkeys asleep, gonna bang on the glass n wake em up | No, banging frightens and stresses them | Yes, they like to see visitors n wave at them | Yes, a little tap does no harm | Yes, if you use the camera flash too
test: ben reckons owls can spin their head right round. true? | No, only about three quarters of the way | Yes, they can turn it all the way | Yes, they turn it twice round | No, they cannot turn their head at all
test: let's burn ants with the magnifying glass, its science | No, that burns and hurts living things | Yes, ants are too small to feel the heat | Yes, if you only do a few | Yes, it is fine because it is science
explain: A man climbed over a barrier at a zoo to get closer to a big cat; it struck him through the fence, and he needed surgery on his arm.
explain: A boy in Ireland took a baby rabbit from a nest in a field to keep as a pet; it was too young to eat on its own, and in two days it died even though he tried to feed it.
fact: Zoo barriers keep people far enough away that an animal cannot reach them; climbing over one has led to serious injuries.
fact: Most young wild animals found alone are not lost; their parent is nearby, and taking them away often kills them.
test: baby bunny in the long grass all alone, can i take it home | No, leave it, its mum comes back and it dies away from her | Yes, it was left behind by its mum and it really needs you now | Yes, just give it some warm milk | Only if you keep it in a shoebox
test: kid climbed over the zoo railing to get closer to the tiger, what can happen | The animal can reach and badly hurt him | The tiger is too sleepy to care | The keepers give him a free ticket | Nothing, zoo animals are tame
test: zoo sign says dont feed the animals, my brother says a few chips is fine | No, people food can make zoo animals ill | Yes, chips are good for monkeys, they love salt | Yes, if you give only a handful | Only feed them if they look hungry
test: some guy climbed into the tiger bit at the zoo and the tiger ripped his arm proper, what's the actual lesson | Stay behind zoo barriers, zoo animals are still wild | Tigers only attack people wearing bright colours | Zoo animals are tame, he must have teased it | Climbing is fine if a keeper is nearby
test: my mate says sharks hunt people at every beach and like 1000 kids get eaten a year, true? | Hugely exaggerated; sharks rarely attack people at all | True, sharks like eating people much more than eating fish | True, but only at beaches in the summer | True, so nobody should ever swim in the sea
test: everyone's sayin Mia's a monster cos she took a baby rabbit and it died, should i spread it | No, it's unkind; she probably did not know, kindly tell her | Yes, everyone must know she is cruel | Yes, but only tell the people in your class | Yes, and add that she did it on purpose to be mean to it
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vision of bats. | Bats are able to see, and many also navigate by echolocation | Bats are wholly blind and rely on smell alone | Bats see solely by means of light reflected from the moon | Bats are blind by day and see only by night
exam: Identify the minimum distance which members of the public are advised to maintain from a seal resting upon a beach. | At least ten metres, with dogs retained on a lead | At least one metre, with dogs permitted to run free | No distance, since seals are wholly harmless | At least ten metres, though dogs may approach freely
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the rotational capacity of an owl's head? | It turns approximately three quarters of the way round | It turns fully round in a complete circle | It does not turn, as the owl moves its eyes instead | It turns only a quarter of the way to each side
exam: Determine the most appropriate course of action for a child who witnesses the deliberate infliction of harm upon an animal. | Inform a trusted adult, who may report it to a welfare organisation | Confront the person responsible and threaten them | Disregard the matter, as animals feel no pain | Remove the animal to one's own home without informing anyone
test: zookeeper said the two swans are "together for life", kids went ewww lol | Swans stay as a pair, like a married couple; it is nature, no need to tease | It means they are glued together | It means one is a baby | It means they fight all the time
test: Jack said "the penguins are in love" n everyone teased Lily cos she smiled | Penguins pair up as couples; Lily smiling is fine, so do not tease her | Lily must be in love with Jack | Penguins can't love anything | Tease Lily more
test: teacher said the lion "has his pride" n Ben goes "he's got loads of girlfriends lol" | A pride is a lion family group; it is a joke, so laugh kindly but do not tease anyone | It means the lion is very proud | It means lions get married | It means Ben is in love
